WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:11.612
Hello, everyone.

00:00:11.612 --> 00:00:16.028
I would like to welcome you to today's
webinar entitled Using Genomics,

00:00:16.028 --> 00:00:19.245
Wearables and
Big Data to Manage Health and Disease.

00:00:19.245 --> 00:00:23.134
My name is Annette Cruz and I'm the
Program Manager of the Stanford Genetics

00:00:23.134 --> 00:00:26.235
and Genomics Certificate Program
at the Stanford Center for

00:00:26.235 --> 00:00:27.768
Professional Development.

00:00:27.768 --> 00:00:30.716
This webinar is brought to you
by the Stanford Center for

00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:35.501
Professional Development and the Stanford
Genetics and Genomics Certificate Program.

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:38.169
Take online courses in genetics and
genomics and

00:00:38.169 --> 00:00:43.676
gain a greater understanding of biology,
human health and personalized medicine.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:47.231
Happened through the world class
research of Stanford faculty and

00:00:47.231 --> 00:00:49.517
industry experts to acquire the skills and

00:00:49.517 --> 00:00:53.532
knowledge you need to succeed in
the rapidly evolving genetics industry.

00:00:53.532 --> 00:00:57.130
Visit geneticscertificate.stanford.edu
to learn more.

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:05.208
Before we begin, I would like to introduce
you to today's featured presenter,

00:01:05.208 --> 00:01:06.592
Dr. Michael Snyder.

00:01:06.592 --> 00:01:10.840
Dr. Snyder is a Stanford Ascherman
Professor and Chair of Genetics and

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.887
the Director of the Center of Genomics and
Personalized Medicine.

00:01:14.887 --> 00:01:19.775
Dr. Snyder received Ph.D training at
the California Institute of Technology and

00:01:19.775 --> 00:01:23.420
carried out postdoctoral
training at Stanford University.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.085
He is the leader in the field
of functional genomics and

00:01:27.085 --> 00:01:31.460
proteomics and one of the major
participants of the ENCODE project.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.980
His laboratory study was the first

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:38.920
to perform a large scale functional
genomics project in any organism.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.484
And has launched many technologies
in genomics and proteomics that have

00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:48.442
been used for characterizing genomes,
proteomes and regulatory networks.

00:01:48.442 --> 00:01:50.489
I would like to turn it over to Mike now.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.860
&gt;&gt; Great, well, thanks very much for
having me on this program.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.647
I look forward to telling you about some
of the things we're up to using data to

00:01:59.647 --> 00:02:01.235
help manage people's health.

00:02:01.235 --> 00:02:05.960
And ideally help manage their,
ideally cure their disease.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:13.910
So this is an area, I think many of you
appreciate, is incredibly hot these days

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:19.040
and very topical and I think one of the
events that really launched this was when

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.320
Obama announced that we're going to have
a major initiative in precision medicine.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:27.300
This was in his inaugural
address in January 2015,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.300
and he pointed to this DNA helix as
the way in which we're going to be able

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.950
to use genomics and information to be
able to better manage people's health.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.377
Well, I would argue this goes
well beyond just genomics.

00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:43.581
I think we're going to see
an explosion of all kinds of

00:02:43.581 --> 00:02:46.561
data that will help manage
people's health and

00:02:46.561 --> 00:02:51.425
predict medicines they should use at
a level that's never been done before.

00:02:51.425 --> 00:02:53.247
Now, before we dive into this,

00:02:53.247 --> 00:02:56.963
let me first show you a little bit
about what frames our thinking.

00:02:56.963 --> 00:03:01.132
Which is that your health,
if you will, it's a state.

00:03:01.132 --> 00:03:04.053
And most of the time,
we're healthy and in a healthy state.

00:03:04.053 --> 00:03:07.766
But there are times in which we get
disease and we become unhealthy.

00:03:07.766 --> 00:03:12.464
When you get a viral infection or
perhaps even other nastier diseases.

00:03:12.464 --> 00:03:15.472
And these states, they are influenced
by many different things.

00:03:15.472 --> 00:03:17.750
They are influenced by your DNA, your so

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.307
called genome which is a collection
of all your DNA molecules.

00:03:21.307 --> 00:03:25.522
And also, the various things you're
exposed to, the so called expose zones.

00:03:25.522 --> 00:03:30.766
That would be the foods you eat,
pathogens you might be exposed to,

00:03:30.766 --> 00:03:33.620
various life stresses, exercise.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.370
We all know intuitively that these
will contribute to your health.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.120
And I would argue that a goal of us
collectively, is to try and understand,

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.050
in quantitative terms,
exactly how people, when they're born

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:50.280
with a certain DNA sequence, when they
are exposed to different molecules.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.840
What kind of health
outcome we might predict?

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.850
And I think this will occur in some,
if you will, probabilistic fashion.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.530
Some means in which we
would be able to predict

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.190
whether someone might be healthy or
get disease.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:07.520
In just as an example,
if you're at risk for Parkinson's,

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.150
you probably don't want
to be a pesticide worker.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.260
Because that makes you at higher risk for
getting Parkinson's.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.340
So, and there are DNA variants
that lead to Parkinson's.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.140
So again I think this is where
we're only we going to head.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.668
I do believe we're in a world where
soon people will be getting their genome

00:04:25.668 --> 00:04:28.010
sequence before they're born.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:32.887
And that will actually make that all the
more important we understand what kinds

00:04:32.887 --> 00:04:36.031
of exposures lead to what
kind of health outcomes.

00:04:36.031 --> 00:04:40.700
Now I think many of you know it's
possible to sequence people's DNA.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.673
So there's been a revolution of DNA
sequencing technology when it's at

00:04:44.673 --> 00:04:48.916
the point where we can now sequence
someone's DNA for less than $2000.

00:04:48.916 --> 00:04:52.008
Although to interpret that DNA
sequence costs a lot more.

00:04:52.008 --> 00:04:55.950
In fact, it can cost as much as 15 or
$20,000.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:59.594
But that's not the only revolution
in technology that's occurring.

00:04:59.594 --> 00:05:04.393
There's other revolutions and
advanced technologies, mass spectrometry,

00:05:04.393 --> 00:05:07.472
the sorts of instruments
that you see in airports,

00:05:07.472 --> 00:05:11.982
there is very advanced instruments that
let us measure tens of thousands of

00:05:11.982 --> 00:05:16.589
molecules inside of us that again at
a level that has never been done before.

00:05:16.589 --> 00:05:20.567
So we can make measurements
of unprecedented scale and

00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:25.333
be able to better define what
people's biochemical states are.

00:05:25.333 --> 00:05:27.022
So a number of years ago,

00:05:27.022 --> 00:05:31.688
we launched a large project which
we call Personal Omics Profiling,

00:05:31.688 --> 00:05:36.045
where the idea is to make very
detailed measurements of people.

00:05:36.045 --> 00:05:39.861
So we make literally billions of
measurements of people every time we

00:05:39.861 --> 00:05:40.664
sample them.

00:05:40.664 --> 00:05:45.812
So we analyze their DNA, that's
their genome, as I mentioned before.

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:49.230
But we also analyze all these
other features as well.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.060
So something called the epigenome,
which is your modified DNA.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.532
Which we know is influenced by the
environment and all those other things I,

00:05:56.532 --> 00:05:58.240
mentioned earlier.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.620
We also measure all your RNA molecules.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.760
This is called your transcriptome.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.270
All your proteins, that's your proteome.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:07.468
Special set of proteins called cytokines.

00:06:07.468 --> 00:06:09.488
They modulate your immune system.

00:06:09.488 --> 00:06:13.600
Then we follow all your metabolites and
small molecules, as well.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.320
Or as many as we can measure.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.680
We follow your antibody responses.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.820
You may have heard about this
new area called microbiome.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:25.150
This is an area where we actually have
many, many cells that live in and

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:29.560
on us that are really microbes that are
actually very beneficial for our health.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.280
They digest our food and
we study that as well.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.520
So we actually measure,
most of these we measure out of blood but

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.290
some we measure out of urine and
the microbiome, we actually measure,

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:42.010
believe it or not,
out of your stool sample.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.210
Between each measurements of all
these different things, again,

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.859
billions of measurements
every time we sample people.

00:06:47.859 --> 00:06:52.848
And the idea is actually to sample people
over time to see what keeps them healthy

00:06:52.848 --> 00:06:56.433
and what makes them,
what goes wrong when they get sick.

00:06:56.433 --> 00:07:00.250
And we do standard medical tests over here
as well, we also have questionnaires.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:05.199
And I'm going to get back to these
wearable sensors at the end of

00:07:05.199 --> 00:07:06.565
the talk today.

00:07:06.565 --> 00:07:08.284
So basically, why are we doing all this?

00:07:08.284 --> 00:07:13.390
Well we're trying to understand why people
are healthy, what keeps them healthy.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:16.730
And by understanding
the biochemical measures of health.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:20.630
We also wonder what happens when they
do get sick and when you do a get

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:25.630
diseases what kinds of biochemical
events change during that time.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:27.974
And then how can we better manage that.

00:07:27.974 --> 00:07:31.484
We want to understand how these different,
I mentioned omes, so

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:34.929
the proteome is your collection
of proteins and the metabolome

00:07:34.929 --> 00:07:39.755
is your collection of metabolites and your
microbiome is your collection of microbes.

00:07:39.755 --> 00:07:42.130
We want to understand how
they relate to one another.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:47.480
We know intuitively that the food you
eat in combination with your genome and

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:52.650
your biochemical marks on your
DNA somehow affect your health.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.510
But we don't really understand
how those rules work and

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:59.830
we'd like to understand how they work so
that we'll be able to Better manage your

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:04.390
health, that is to say, that certain foods
can make you better by eating them, and

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:06.420
they will be specific for
some individuals.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.910
We like people to eat those foods and

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:13.850
not foods that make you unhealthy,
and this can be very personalized.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.290
We're also trying to understand
how people react similarly and

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:23.290
how they react differently when you're
faced with, say, adverse conditions.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:29.320
If you have a virus that comes along,
most people respond somewhat similarly,

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.760
in the sense that they'll
mount an immune response.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.540
But there will be personal
aspects to this, as well.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:37.400
Some people will make certain cytokines,
and

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:40.210
be able to better fend off that virus and
others, not so good.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.590
And we'd like to understand
why that's the case.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.640
And perhaps, most importantly,
one major reason we're doing this

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.590
project is we're trying to
identify factors that can,

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.470
basically, help manage
the health of an individual.

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:58.050
We can learn things about people that
we hope will keep them healthy, and

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.030
you'll see evidence of
that as we go along.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.570
So, basically, many of you may
have known this already, but

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.360
we actually started this
project by studying me.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.349
Looks like we lost some information here.

00:09:12.349 --> 00:09:18.165
But, basically, we've been profiling
me for, now, over six and a half years,

00:09:18.165 --> 00:09:24.427
meaning we've taken over 200 time points
from me, many samples at each time point.

00:09:24.427 --> 00:09:27.642
And during this time,
I've been through ten viral infections,

00:09:27.642 --> 00:09:29.110
I've also had lyme disease.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.290
And we basically have,
as I say, been analyzing me and

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.930
following me very closely
over this period.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:38.541
And we did this because we wanted to see,
again,

00:09:38.541 --> 00:09:43.487
what happens when I'm healthy and
what goes wrong when I get sick.

00:09:43.487 --> 00:09:46.770
So every one of these boxes
is a viral infection.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:49.078
It's supposed to be a timeline,
here, that somehow got lost.

00:09:49.078 --> 00:09:54.449
But, basically, during every one of these
viral infections, I have gotten sick.

00:09:54.449 --> 00:09:56.790
And we've take many, many samples.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:59.960
So, for example,
these are all common colds I've had.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.652
This is a rhinovirus, HRV.

00:10:01.652 --> 00:10:05.830
This is a respiratory syncytial virus,
that's the common cold in kids.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.490
Adenovirus is another one.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:11.120
And so we do a dense sampling
when I get a viral infection.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.270
And then we typically give blood every
two to three months when I'm healthy.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:18.150
And lately I've been trying different
dietary supplements that we've been

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:18.930
sampling a lot.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.238
Each of these is a day number,
each of these numbers.

00:10:21.238 --> 00:10:22.234
And so, again,

00:10:22.234 --> 00:10:26.450
we're following what happens as I
go through these various changes.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:30.310
And the first thing we did
was actually sequence my DNA.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:34.120
I'll go through this somewhat quickly
because I'd like to talk about some new,

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:34.820
exciting stuff.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:39.420
But from my DNA sequence, we could predict
my risk for many different diseases.

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:43.760
And most of this I knew
from my family history or

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:48.390
I know about myself already,
meaning I was at low risk for obesity.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:49.989
Nobody in my family has
ever been overweight.

00:10:49.989 --> 00:10:54.872
I was at high risk for what's called
dyslipidemia and high cholesterol,

00:10:54.872 --> 00:10:57.720
that shows up here as high triglycerides.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.346
I was at high risk for those.

00:10:59.346 --> 00:11:03.185
I knew that already because I
knew my cholesterol was high, and

00:11:03.185 --> 00:11:05.220
I do take statins to manage that.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.046
High risk for coronary artery disease,
that was done as well.

00:11:08.046 --> 00:11:11.683
One of the big surprises from my genome,
though, was I was at high risk for

00:11:11.683 --> 00:11:12.639
type 2 diabetes.

00:11:12.639 --> 00:11:16.610
Basically, I went from having
a 27% chance of having

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:21.490
this in my lifetime to a 46% chance,
so a huge increase.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:24.139
And we actually were following my glucose,

00:11:24.139 --> 00:11:28.300
which is what happens when you get
diabetes, your glucose goes off.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.798
And, in fact, that did happen to
me during the course of the study.

00:11:31.798 --> 00:11:34.049
My glucose had been
marching along just fine.

00:11:34.049 --> 00:11:38.332
Then it actually turns out right
after this nasty respiratory

00:11:38.332 --> 00:11:42.298
syncytial virus infection,
my glucose went quite off.

00:11:42.298 --> 00:11:46.860
It got very high to the point where I was,
in fact, classified as diabetic.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:51.320
So when we first measured,
it was on its way up at 127,

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.230
a week later it was even higher.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:57.116
This is a better measure for steady state
glucose, it's called hemoglobin A1C.

00:11:57.116 --> 00:11:59.017
It was right on the edge, it's arbitrary.

00:11:59.017 --> 00:12:01.756
But diabetes is classified as 6.5,

00:12:01.756 --> 00:12:05.160
hemoglobin A1C at a 6.4
is right on the edge.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:09.905
And then five weeks later,
we measure me again and, sure enough,

00:12:09.905 --> 00:12:11.783
I was actually diabetic.

00:12:11.783 --> 00:12:14.202
I had crossed the threshold, I was 6.7.

00:12:14.202 --> 00:12:17.350
And that was over five years ago,
that was April 11,

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:20.924
2011 because up until then I
had the world's worst diet.

00:12:20.924 --> 00:12:25.110
I ate cake, sweets,
I didn't know I had a problem.

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:28.093
I cut all that out,
I doubled my biking and started running.

00:12:28.093 --> 00:12:31.480
And it took about eight months, but
I brought it down under control.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:33.460
I got my glucose back together.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:37.500
So this made a big splash
at the time because it's

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.680
the first time someone used their genome
to predict their risk for a disease.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.450
In my case, I got the disease.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:48.210
And in my case it was also manageable, and

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:51.520
I was able to bring it under control,
at least initially.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.296
And it was also quite intriguing
because if notice when it occurred,

00:12:55.296 --> 00:12:59.550
it occurred right after this nasty
respiratory syncytial virus infection.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:03.756
So what we think is that I'm genetically
predisposed for type 2 diabetes, and

00:13:03.756 --> 00:13:06.770
then in conjunction with this
nasty viral infection, or

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.816
its associated stress response,
that's what's triggered the disease.

00:13:10.816 --> 00:13:16.529
Now, you may think that this all has a
happy ending, and it's not completely so.

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:21.065
It turns out that some people,
in fact, criticize and say maybe,

00:13:21.065 --> 00:13:25.602
Mike, that would have gone away
on its own, you don't know that.

00:13:25.602 --> 00:13:29.502
But we accidentally did the right
control experiment, so to speak.

00:13:29.502 --> 00:13:34.483
That is to say, we kept profiling me,
but we were doing so blindly.

00:13:34.483 --> 00:13:37.796
It was actually when we scaled,
you'll see in a minute,

00:13:37.796 --> 00:13:40.497
that we now follow 100
people just like me.

00:13:40.497 --> 00:13:44.424
And when we scaled,
we had stopped looking at the data, so

00:13:44.424 --> 00:13:46.360
we were doing this blindly.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:50.374
And then at this point here somebody
looked at my hemoglobin A1C,

00:13:50.374 --> 00:13:55.979
which turned out to be 7.0, and said,
Mike, you're 7.0, you're diabetic again.

00:13:55.979 --> 00:13:58.313
I said, I had no idea, so they sampled me.

00:13:58.313 --> 00:14:00.756
I'd stopped running, so I started running,

00:14:00.756 --> 00:14:03.770
and I could bring it a little
bit under control to 5.7.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:07.830
But these days it's actually drifted up,

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:12.350
in fact,
when I got Lyme disease it hit 6.5.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.730
And it's gone up high again recently.

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:18.060
So these things,
it hovers between 6.2 and 6.4.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:23.050
There's no question I have
a metabolic disorder, and

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:26.600
it's not fully under control because I'm
right on the verge of being diabetic.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.865
So, again, this is hemoglobin A1C,
this is glucose, and

00:14:29.865 --> 00:14:32.730
I'm way elevated over what I should be.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:36.403
And it's just not me, my brothers and
sisters all have elevated glucose.

00:14:36.403 --> 00:14:39.434
We're all fairly thin, so
it's a little bit surprising.

00:14:39.434 --> 00:14:44.332
But it's pretty clear, then,
that we have a genetic predisposition for

00:14:44.332 --> 00:14:45.598
type 2 diabetes.

00:14:45.598 --> 00:14:48.164
And that's what the first
one is with type 2.

00:14:48.164 --> 00:14:53.524
So basically, again,
this is a case where genomics and

00:14:53.524 --> 00:14:57.870
genetics actually predicted disease risk.

00:14:57.870 --> 00:14:59.875
In my case,
I could always partially manage it.

00:14:59.875 --> 00:15:04.115
I'll probably be doing other
things like taking drugs to get my

00:15:04.115 --> 00:15:06.677
glucose under control in the future.

00:15:06.677 --> 00:15:07.831
Now, since that time,

00:15:07.831 --> 00:15:10.852
we've actually carried out
other kinds of assays, as well.

00:15:10.852 --> 00:15:15.852
We did an IMAX movie-type
picture of my biochemistry

00:15:15.852 --> 00:15:22.420
as it changes during the time I get
sick to when we got type 2 diabetes.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:25.820
But we've actually been making
other measurements, as well.

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:31.018
One very fascinating area is the field
of epigenetics and epigenomics.

00:15:31.018 --> 00:15:36.382
Epigenomics is a collection of your
modifications to your chromosomes,

00:15:36.382 --> 00:15:37.336
if you will.

00:15:37.336 --> 00:15:41.602
And one important set of
modifications is there's chemical

00:15:41.602 --> 00:15:44.534
modifications called DNA methylation.

00:15:44.534 --> 00:15:49.372
So what's interesting is that
we know that your DNA can get

00:15:49.372 --> 00:15:53.930
modified in response to
various environmental cues.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:57.730
But we don't fully understand it,
a very fascinating field.

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:02.101
But we know that this one in particular,
modification of your DNA by

00:16:02.101 --> 00:16:05.858
genome methylation,
which is a chemical modification,

00:16:05.858 --> 00:16:10.690
we know it's affected by nutrition and
affected by things like exercise,

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:16.235
even your age and other environmental cues
all affect your epigenetic programming.

00:16:16.235 --> 00:16:21.713
We also know that it's associated with
modification with turning off your genes,

00:16:21.713 --> 00:16:27.736
so we don't how the whole thing works,
but we do know it's very, very important,

00:16:27.736 --> 00:16:33.350
and so, the things you do will directly
affect your gene and your health.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:38.300
And there are now ways of mapping out
all your modified DNA sites, something

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.420
called whole genome bisulfate sequencing,
deferrals, in this case, I know.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:45.830
But you can actually map out all
of the modifications in your DNA,

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:49.060
and there a millions of
them using this technology.

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:53.490
So, we've done this for me,
and it's quite fascinating,

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:57.340
you can see very interesting reads
into the genome that change.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:17:00.370
One area that we think is
interesting is trying to use your

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:05.090
DNA modification to predict what
diseases you might be at risk for,

00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:10.060
just like we used your genome sequencing
to predict what diseases you might catch.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:14.560
We can also look at your modified
DNA to try and do that, as well,

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:17.730
to understand what diseases
you might be at risk for.

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:18.880
And this was just one example.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:21.380
I have a number of regions
of the genome like this,

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:25.810
that are affected both genetically and
epigenetically.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:29.410
So, this is a case where I have
a change in this particular gene.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.920
It's called a phosphodiesterase gene.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.840
And the point out of all
this is that this gene,

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:39.160
turns out my father's copy is
actually inactivated genetically.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:42.670
We all have these kinds of mutations,
and then in my case,

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:48.510
my father's gene has actually been
inactivated through a genetic change.

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:54.760
And my mother's copy is actually shut off
by epigenetics, so the control sequences

00:17:54.760 --> 00:18:00.840
are modified by this DNA methylation I
mentioned earlier, until we get very few,

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:05.558
very little activity from this gene,
which we can measure by certain readouts,

00:18:05.558 --> 00:18:09.690
it's called RNAC printing,
we get very few RNAs that come from this.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:14.792
So, the point out of all this is that
this gene is then activated in two ways,

00:18:14.792 --> 00:18:18.150
one epigenetically, and one genetically.

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:23.650
And as a consequence, I actually have
this again, inaccurate about two methods.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:26.670
And it fact, I make more eosinophils,

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:28.640
which are involved in
your immune response.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:30.350
I make more of these
than I normally should.

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:34.990
So, we think that getting your genome
sequence is not only important, but

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:39.450
we also would argue that getting your
epigenome determined as well is going to

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:42.940
be just as important, possibly even
more so, because that's something

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:47.430
you might be able to modify through the
food you eat and your exercise and such.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:50.470
Well, as I mentioned before,

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:53.750
we've actually scaled this now
to study 100 different people.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:56.960
We're following 100 people in
incredible detail, just like me.

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:00.460
We sample them every three
months when they're healthy, and

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:05.220
if something unfortunate happens,
like a viral infection or stress response,

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:07.130
we'll sample them more frequently.

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:09.040
We'll take about five or so

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:13.700
extra time points to see what
happens when people get sick.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.130
We've also been running people through
some of these dietary protobations,

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:22.300
these dietary changes to see
the affect of diet on people's health.

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:28.320
And so again, we've been doing this
in incredible detail to see exactly

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:32.040
what happens when people take supplements
or certain other kinds of dietary changes.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:36.640
And so, we've actually been doing this for
now over three years,

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.730
we've collected this,
where we're at the beginning of summer,

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:43.180
we've collected over 1500
time points from people.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:46.600
So, we're really learning
in incredible detail how

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:50.220
people are when they're healthy, and
what happens when they do get sick.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.780
And just to give you a few examples of
some of the things we learned from this,

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:57.950
it turns out we learned important
health information from almost

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.410
every single person we've studied.

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:05.160
So, just to give you some examples
from the genome sequence for

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:10.120
the first 100 people, we've looked at
50 in detail, and three of them have

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:13.650
mutations in their DNA that are very very
important for them to know about, so.

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:18.950
One individual had a mutation in this
gene which puts her at high frequency,

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:23.650
or high risk for
this type of cancer called paraganglioma.

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:28.840
And because she had her genome sequenced,
a simple urine test to keep an eye out for

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:32.200
that, which she would not have
done had we not sequenced her DNA.

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:35.690
She would have no idea that
she was at risk for this.

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:38.000
Another individual has
a protein C mutation,

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.740
which puts him at high risk for
coagulation.

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:46.004
And a third person actually has a mutation
that affects their heart rhythm,

00:20:46.004 --> 00:20:49.704
and possibility of heart attacks or
heart failures,

00:20:49.704 --> 00:20:52.844
something called dilated cardiomyopathy.

00:20:52.844 --> 00:20:57.021
So they have a high risk for this, which
got discovered partly through their family

00:20:57.021 --> 00:21:00.390
history, and
partly because we sequenced their DNA.

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:04.358
And in fact, they do have some hard issues
that they're taking medications for.

00:21:04.358 --> 00:21:09.532
So, just from their DNA alone from
three people turned out to be very,

00:21:09.532 --> 00:21:14.881
very useful information about things
they should do to keep an eye out for

00:21:14.881 --> 00:21:16.740
possible disease risk.

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:20.630
And so, again, they will use this
information to better manage your health.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:26.303
It turns out we're also trying to
understand what happens to people when

00:21:26.303 --> 00:21:28.534
they gain and when they lose weight, so.

00:21:28.534 --> 00:21:32.950
I think many of you, in fact, some of you
may be even experiencing this when people

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:37.240
gain too much weight, and then [INAUDIBLE]
what does that mean biochemically?

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:40.340
Well, we have looked at those,
another group of folks,

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:44.151
many of them I forgot to mention,
but they are actually prediabetic,

00:21:44.151 --> 00:21:48.158
and there's a certain type of
prediabetes called insulin resistances,

00:21:48.158 --> 00:21:50.830
it puts people at high risk for
type 2 diabetes.

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:54.660
Most of these folks will
get type two diabetes.

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:58.380
So we've been following them in detail

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:02.370
through the same kind of
profiling that I have mentioned.

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:05.830
And as they say, this group of 24
people went through a weight gain,

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:10.050
weight loss study, and half of them
are high-risk for type 2 diabetes,

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.220
the other half are normal healthy folks.

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:14.980
And they're matched for their height and

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:19.290
weight, called body mass index,
they're matched for that.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:19.880
And so, basically,

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:24.380
we wanted to see what happens to these
folks when they gain or lose weight.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.910
And what we discovered,
first of all, even at baseline,

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:31.080
even before they gain or lose weight,
we can tell the prediabetics

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:34.380
apart from the diabetics based
on their molecular composition.

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:37.490
So, this is by looking at
all their RNA molecules.

00:22:37.490 --> 00:22:41.020
It turns out the insulin resistant
folks are more elevated for

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:45.270
certain kinds of biochemical pathways,
like oxidative phosphorylation.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:48.010
They actually have more of a certain
platelets, specific genes that

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:51.435
are stressed and some other diabetes
related genes that are often

00:22:51.435 --> 00:22:56.240
prediabetic relative to the insulin
sensitive, these are the healthy folks.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:22:59.650
And the same is true for metabolites.

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:02.930
I showed you the RNA, but
here's the metabolites.

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:06.292
So, it turns out the insulin
resistant folks,

00:23:06.292 --> 00:23:10.665
the prediabetics are more likely
to have certain metabolites

00:23:10.665 --> 00:23:15.960
elevated relative to the sensitive
folks that have other ones.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:22.200
It correlates very sharply with their
likelihood to get type 2 diabetes,

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:25.600
and so, what's interesting about
this metabolite is it actually

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:28.250
comes from your microbiome of all things.

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:32.590
So, the point out of all this is that your
microcomposition of people who are at risk

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:35.420
for type 2 diabetes and
those who aren't are very, very different.

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:39.710
What we found though is that as
people gain and lose weight,

00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:42.460
their biochemistry does
change dramatically.

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:44.760
So, these are the molecules
that do change.

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:49.390
Every row is a different molecule, and
every column, this is at the beginning,

00:23:49.390 --> 00:23:52.600
this is after they gain weight,
this is after they lose weight.

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:54.950
So up here,
the molecules will start out low, and

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:57.830
then they get high when people gain
weight, and they go back to low.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:01.660
And there are other molecules that are
high when they're healthy, and when they

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:06.190
gain weight they actually, these molecules
go down and then they return to normal.

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:10.070
And then there are other interesting
ones that makes their high go low but

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:10.840
actually stay low.

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:13.650
They don't quite come back and
we can actually

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:17.800
identify these patterns systematically and
see the pathways to change.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:21.420
And the bottom line out of all this is
that when you're gaining weight actually,

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:25.950
one interesting finding was that
your inflammation response goes up.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:29.230
So your immune response gets
activated when you gain weight and

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:31.280
then it returns to normal
after you lose weight.

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:34.280
Another interesting pathway that changes,

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:37.780
it turns out, is when your
hypertrophic cardiomyopathy pathway.

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:42.690
That's a lot of mumbo-jumbo, but what that
means is that this pathway gets activated,

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:46.740
which puts you at a very high risk for
heart attack when you gain weight.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:49.570
So we have a biochemical
understanding now,

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:53.150
how people might get heart
attacks when they gain weight.

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:56.628
It comes from the fact that those pathways
activated when you gained weight.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.750
We've also found what turns
out to be really fascinating.

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:02.360
We found that even at baseline,

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:07.920
even when people are healthy at baseline,
their profiles are very, very different.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:11.520
So these are some very fascinating
immune molecules called cytokines.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:15.470
It turns out we all have different
levels of these cytokines,

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:17.890
even the baseline when we are healthy.

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:22.900
This is a complicated figure but
each color is a different person.

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:29.900
Each name here around the outside
is the name of the cytokine.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:33.690
This is high levels and
this is low levels.

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:37.461
What we found for examples, of the blue
person has a lot of this one, IL7.

00:25:37.461 --> 00:25:40.530
The light brown person has IP10.

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:44.210
The orange person has this one MIP1B and
so on and so forth.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:47.790
We all have different levels of cytokine
which probably puts us at risk for

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:49.690
certain diseases differently.

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:53.010
So some of us,
if I were to throw a virus on a room and

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:57.080
half the people might get sick and half
might not, then that might be because we

00:25:57.080 --> 00:26:00.440
have different levels of cytokine
that help us fend off those diseases.

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:05.232
As it also turns out, you've heard
about your microbiome perhaps.

00:26:05.232 --> 00:26:08.880
Your microbiome is very, very important.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:15.080
It's the collection of microbes in and
on you and a lot of them are in your gut.

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:18.250
And it's thought that we have
ten times as many bacteria

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:21.870
in our gut as we do human
cells in our whole body.

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:24.810
And so the microbiome is essential for

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:28.940
digesting your food and making essential
vitamins and things like this.

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:30.960
It's a very, very important good thing.

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:34.090
It's not always bad like
you might be worried about.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:37.138
But it turns out,
if we look at people's microbiome,

00:26:37.138 --> 00:26:39.670
everybody's microbiome is different.

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:44.050
That's been known for some time that
we all have different microbiome but

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:45.890
some people can be different
in interesting ways.

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:51.110
This is two people here who eat a lot
of almonds and they're very different.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:56.150
This is a way of relating people
to one another in this plot.

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:58.365
If you're similar,
you'll be very close to each other.

00:26:58.365 --> 00:27:00.900
Well these two individuals
eat a lot of almonds.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:04.040
It's a family, the husband and
wife, they eat a lot of almonds and

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:06.140
they were just very different
from everyone else.

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:10.930
So your diet can really shape your
microbiome and long term changes.

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:16.200
What we found is that for
short term perturbations,

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:20.160
like the weight gain study I mentioned or
viral infections, you will shift your

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:25.880
microbiome but you actually look closest
to your self rather than someone else.

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:29.673
So this is just comparing people
in different time points.

00:27:29.673 --> 00:27:31.944
Every color is a different person and

00:27:31.944 --> 00:27:36.925
it turns out the people are like this red
person, if we ask who's closest to who,

00:27:36.925 --> 00:27:40.294
well even if you gain weight or
have a viral infection,

00:27:40.294 --> 00:27:45.010
your microbiome will look closest to
yourself, rather than someone else.

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:50.050
So the point out of all this is that we
all have personal microbiomes that shift

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:54.690
when we do gain weight, or
when we get a viral infection, and

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.040
it's a short term perturbation.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.720
It will shift but you'll still look
closest to you, your bacteria and

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:01.270
things are very personalized.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:05.360
And that's probably because we tend to
eat very similar diets and our genome and

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:09.560
epigenome help control all of this.

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:11.740
So this is really where I
think all this is going.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:15.589
I see a world where we'll be
able to understand where people,

00:28:15.589 --> 00:28:20.401
when they're faced with different
perturbation, so some people if they eat

00:28:20.401 --> 00:28:24.563
unhealthy they will gain weight and
become metabolically a mess.

00:28:24.563 --> 00:28:30.260
Other people, if they eat unhealthy, they
actually may gain weight or they may not.

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:34.230
But, even if they gain weight,
they can stay metabolically healthy.

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:38.010
There are plenty of vary obese people
who don't have Type 2 Diabetes and

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:43.010
we'd like to understand biochemically why
that is so we can better manage people.

00:28:43.010 --> 00:28:46.570
Certainly we can try and
make sure that they eat healthy foods but

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:50.840
we should make sure that they
eat healthy foods for them.

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:55.730
And the food can be very specific for
individuals.

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:57.890
We're also trying to
understand how certain people,

00:28:57.890 --> 00:29:01.520
when they get viral infections, some
get sick but some others stay healthy.

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:02.990
And, again, we'd like to try and

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:07.130
keep people as healthy as possible
from these circumstances.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.240
This isn't the only study we're running.

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:12.160
We're running another one,

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:15.790
where once a year we're profiling
people from around the world.

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:20.850
Actually it is part of a meeting
that I am involved in,

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:23.550
it is called
the Human Proteome Organization.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.576
We actually are, part of this we
actually go around and sample people and

00:29:27.576 --> 00:29:29.890
follow their biochemical composition.

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:32.960
We do this once a year
at the annual meeting.

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:37.900
And, so we'll be running around the world
picking out crops of people to see

00:29:37.900 --> 00:29:40.280
how biochemically different
they are from one another.

00:29:41.290 --> 00:29:45.500
There's other projects out there as well,
I've just listed a few here.

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:49.880
So, there's one in Seattle run by
Lee Hood, another in Sweden, and so

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:50.530
on and so forth.

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:54.080
There's a number of projects now
erupting like this around the world.

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:58.530
Where people are trying to understand
biochemical detail, what it means

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:03.370
to be healthy and
what happens when people get sick.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:08.600
There's also a new exciting area that's
going to play into this in a big way and

00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:13.150
this is using wearables and sensors to be
able to better manage people's health.

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:16.960
So I mentioned all the biochemical
measurements but there's now getting to be

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:21.530
devices that let you measure many, many
different physiological sorts of things.

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:26.378
In fact, believe it or not,
there's over 500 health related devices

00:30:26.378 --> 00:30:30.691
on the market that let you measure
all kinds of different things.

00:30:30.691 --> 00:30:32.433
And I don't have time to
summarize them all but

00:30:32.433 --> 00:30:33.780
I'll tell you about a few of these.

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:36.180
The simple ones are things like scales

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.410
that you can stand on that will
actually measure your weight.

00:30:39.410 --> 00:30:42.600
And they will send that
information into your smart phone.

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:46.910
There are continuous glucose monitors
which is especially useful for

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:49.420
type one and type two diabetics.

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:53.780
These devices actually prick onto you and

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:56.199
they will continuously
measure your glucose.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:01.370
Probably the most popular device that
you're aware of, the smart watches,

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:03.660
there's well over 50
available on the market.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:06.060
They measure all kinds
of interesting things.

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:09.400
They measure your activity,
how many steps, running, biking.

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:10.980
They'll measure skin temperature.

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:13.560
They can measure,
it depends on the device, but

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:16.430
they can measure your sleep,
stress responses, and so on.

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:18.160
They measure all kinds of things.

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:22.240
And they're measuring, again,
in a continuous fashion.

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:25.700
And what's important is that these
devices collect information and

00:31:25.700 --> 00:31:30.520
they relay that back into your smart
phone and what that means is in

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:34.800
the future your smart phone is going
to be your nexus of health information.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:38.860
All this information is going
to relay back into you,

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:41.190
into your smart phone, and collect this.

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:45.720
And to be honest, I use eight of these
devices everyday to collect information.

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:51.510
It totals up to about two million data
points per day that you can collect and

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.460
even if you don't have time for
all of those devices,

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:56.520
you can actually download
free apps on your iPhone.

00:31:56.520 --> 00:32:01.501
And get some of these as well,
this is one I use.

00:32:01.501 --> 00:32:05.609
It's a free app that lets you
measure your steps, your biking,

00:32:05.609 --> 00:32:09.810
your running and also tells you
where you are all at the same time.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:13.734
So again this information will
all collect into your iPhone and

00:32:13.734 --> 00:32:16.410
help manage it, so why is this important?

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:20.952
Well if you think about it,
you probably go to a doctor at most,

00:32:20.952 --> 00:32:23.281
once a year if you're healthy.

00:32:23.281 --> 00:32:27.880
And they probably see you for about 15
minutes, maybe 30 minutes if you're lucky.

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:33.100
And so out of the 370,000
waking minutes in a year.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:38.070
The doctor just sees you for just
the tiniest possible fraction of them,

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:41.820
a tiny drop in a very, very large bucket.

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:46.669
So we think there's value to actually
being able to measure people all the time,

00:32:46.669 --> 00:32:48.023
collect a lot of data,

00:32:48.023 --> 00:32:52.800
and a lot of information about a person
over a long period and not just in that 15

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:57.330
minutes where you wonder if that's
representative of your health state.

00:32:57.330 --> 00:33:01.557
But by collecting this all the time, you
really get a pretty good idea of what your

00:33:01.557 --> 00:33:05.120
baseline heart rate is and
if it's changing and this sort of thing.

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:10.740
I can tell you personally, what this is
going to mean is that, in the future,

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.820
your smartphone, it's really
going to be your command center,

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:17.390
your control center for
collecting all this information.

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:21.170
And you [INAUDIBLE] biochemical
information in here too and

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:23.410
that will be able to help you
better manage your health.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:28.460
To give you a personal experience out of
this, when I got Lyme disease last summer

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:35.030
in 2015, my wearables were actually
incredibly valuable for discovering this.

00:33:35.030 --> 00:33:37.847
In fact because I discovered that my
oxygen level is lower than it was

00:33:37.847 --> 00:33:39.170
supposed to be.

00:33:39.170 --> 00:33:43.620
My heart rate was elevated and
I knew things weren't quite right.

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:45.860
And then I got a low grade fever for
four days and

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:48.870
in the end I was able to figure
out it was Lyme disease.

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:52.510
I was in Norway so it's a big deal,
they don't have much Lyme disease there.

00:33:52.510 --> 00:33:55.470
In fact the person recommended
I got penicillin and

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.210
I told them I think I need doxicyclin.

00:33:58.210 --> 00:33:59.670
And if I hadn't had the wearables,

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:03.420
first of all the wearables helped
me find this in the first place.

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:09.250
And then by taking command of my situation
I was actually able to get the right drug.

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:13.330
So, I think that's going to be one
of many examples in the future where

00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:17.390
these wearables will feed directly
to you and then you'll use them and

00:34:17.390 --> 00:34:20.380
go to the doctor to help
better manage your health.

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:23.600
And so, we'll not only have
biochemical measurements,

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:26.030
we'll have all these physiological
measurements as well.

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:28.550
So, this is Mike Snyder's future.

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:31.690
I envision a world where people will be
getting their genome sequenced before

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:32.770
they're born.

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:34.780
In addition to all these
biochemical measures,

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:39.470
we're going to have wearable information
as well that all feeds back.

00:34:39.470 --> 00:34:43.070
Again ideally it would feed back to
real time release and short time.

00:34:43.070 --> 00:34:46.040
And with this, we'll be able to
better predict disease risk,

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:48.760
catch disease early, monitor disease and

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:53.280
treat it all based on all this
information, the so-called big data.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:57.380
And the nice part about it is I hope in
the future, we'll be shifting medicine

00:34:57.380 --> 00:35:02.295
from being a reactive situation,
where we react when people get sick.

00:35:02.295 --> 00:35:04.617
To proactive where we work
to keep people healthy.

00:35:04.617 --> 00:35:06.850
That's really the goal.

00:35:06.850 --> 00:35:11.060
And so again, here's where we're at,
personal genome sequencing is here.

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:14.100
All these other multi-omics
profiling's really important.

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:17.920
I think the longitudinal
profiles are very, very valuable.

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:22.170
Everyone is unique, so everyone's special,
not just to the mother.

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:26.240
They have different biochemical profiles.

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:31.760
And I think we are all going to be
responsible for our own health.

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:35.390
So that's wrapping it up,
I'm going to turn back over to Annette and

00:35:36.430 --> 00:35:38.760
yeah happy to take questions
if people have any.

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:41.120
We have a wonderful lab and
wonderful collaborators.

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:44.177
These are the people who
helped out in the study.

00:35:44.177 --> 00:35:45.552
So, back to you Annette.

00:35:45.552 --> 00:35:47.008
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks Mike.

00:35:47.008 --> 00:35:50.059
So, before we do some quick questions,

00:35:50.059 --> 00:35:55.105
I'd like to tell you a little bit
about our program and our center.

00:35:55.105 --> 00:35:59.448
So, the Stanford Center for Professional
Development makes it possible for today's

00:35:59.448 --> 00:36:03.435
best and brightest professionals to
enroll in Stanford University courses and

00:36:03.435 --> 00:36:05.772
programs while they
maintain their careers.

00:36:05.772 --> 00:36:09.594
Curriculum from Stanford departments
is delivered online at Stanford,

00:36:09.594 --> 00:36:12.491
at company work sites, and
international locations,

00:36:12.491 --> 00:36:16.622
providing a global community of learners
with flexibility and convenience and

00:36:16.622 --> 00:36:20.190
enabling them to apply their
education to their work.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:22.099
Additionally the Stanford Center for

00:36:22.099 --> 00:36:25.797
Professional Development has a long
history of extending the world class

00:36:25.797 --> 00:36:29.986
teaching and research of Stanford faculty
to industry corporations of all sizes.

00:36:29.986 --> 00:36:32.883
From start ups to mature
of successful enterprises.

00:36:32.883 --> 00:36:37.981
Visit scpd.stanford.edu to learn more.

00:36:37.981 --> 00:36:41.132
So now we have a few questions, so Mike,

00:36:41.132 --> 00:36:44.765
when will this become
available to everyone?

00:36:44.765 --> 00:36:48.522
&gt;&gt; Well right now it's a research
project so the way we carry it out,

00:36:48.522 --> 00:36:51.321
it really is just for
a limited number of people,

00:36:51.321 --> 00:36:55.270
a hundred folks we're following
in incredible detail.

00:36:55.270 --> 00:36:57.640
But I think some form of this
will come out in the future.

00:36:57.640 --> 00:36:59.880
Obviously, the wearables are there now.

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:03.310
I think we're going to see, they're
starting to be some companies forming in

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:08.050
this area about measuring a lot more data,
to be able to manage people's health.

00:37:08.050 --> 00:37:10.170
So I'm guessing, so

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:15.470
some levels rolling out now, it's not
covered In a typical doctor's appointment.

00:37:15.470 --> 00:37:17.820
But it wouldn't surprise me
if from five to ten years,

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:20.590
we'll see a lot of this activity.

00:37:20.590 --> 00:37:22.500
Certainly, by ten years,
I think we'll see a lot.

00:37:22.500 --> 00:37:26.530
It won't be quite as deep as what we're
doing on me and these 100 people.

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:29.640
But I think it will still
have a lot of data to

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:31.630
be able to better manage people's health.

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:33.038
And I didn't mention but imaging data,

00:37:33.038 --> 00:37:35.490
some of these other things are going to
be very, very important as well.

00:37:37.305 --> 00:37:41.622
&gt;&gt; Great, so with that thought, is that
such a thing as too much information?

00:37:41.622 --> 00:37:47.933
&gt;&gt; Well from my standpoint my answer is
no, some people maybe overwhelmed by this.

00:37:47.933 --> 00:37:53.460
But I think you can manage the kind of
information that you want to come to you.

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:55.573
So yes it's billions of data points but

00:37:55.573 --> 00:37:59.863
in the end it always distills into a few
common themes like either you're sick or

00:37:59.863 --> 00:38:02.440
you're healthy or
you have a viral infection.

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:04.580
Maybe you have Lyme disease.

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:07.650
So it won't be like you'll get
flooded with billions of data points.

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:13.880
You'll get filled with possible things
that you should be on the alert for.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:16.970
And some people know they
won't want this information.

00:38:16.970 --> 00:38:19.310
You don't want to wake up
every morning and worry.

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:21.540
I'm going to get Lyme disease,
or I'm going to get cancer.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:24.320
That would not be a very
healthy situation.

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:29.280
So if you're a worrier and you're worried
about this stuff, my recommendation would

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:33.290
be that you just limit the information
that comes back to you.

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:38.980
Only, it would pop up if it seems a very
strong likelihood that this'll happen,

00:38:38.980 --> 00:38:41.820
or there are certain simple
tests you should be doing.

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:45.040
So I think that threshold of what
information you want to come back

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:47.620
to you will differ for different people.

00:38:47.620 --> 00:38:51.810
And again worriers should probably
get less information, people who can

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:56.130
handle lots of information, by all means
let them have whatever they can handle.

00:38:57.420 --> 00:39:02.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah great so with all the information,
and all the different devices,

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:07.560
and all these different collection
of data What about the privacy?

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:09.648
Who owns all of this information?

00:39:09.648 --> 00:39:13.238
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well at the end of the day
I think you're the owner of your own

00:39:13.238 --> 00:39:14.788
information in my opinion.

00:39:14.788 --> 00:39:19.237
Now you do have to be coached
about this in a sense that we do

00:39:19.237 --> 00:39:24.156
have genetic counselors involved
who let people know that they

00:39:24.156 --> 00:39:28.550
may learn things they don't
want to hear about and such.

00:39:28.550 --> 00:39:30.700
So we do want to manage that.

00:39:30.700 --> 00:39:33.990
But as far as privacy in terms of
insurance companies and things,

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:38.020
at least in the US, you're not allowed
to be discriminated for employment or

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:39.600
health information.

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:42.660
Although, you can for long term
disability and for life insurance.

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:49.448
So those two, you can be discriminated
against so that is a possible concern.

00:39:49.448 --> 00:39:54.260
I would still argue it's better
to know the information and

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:59.300
stay healthy and possibly pay a little
more for life insurance if you have to.

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:04.200
That would be better than the alternative
which is to be unhealthy and not know.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:05.820
So it is a trade off.

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:08.498
Unfortunately, it'd be nice, obviously,

00:40:08.498 --> 00:40:13.385
if there wasn't any discrimination on
those things, but it's somewhat limited.

00:40:13.385 --> 00:40:18.477
I haven't heard any strong cases of
insurance companies discriminating or

00:40:18.477 --> 00:40:24.060
people discriminating against others
based on their genetic information.

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:28.230
Although, it is conceivable it
could happen in the future.

00:40:28.230 --> 00:40:34.142
But yeah, I think again, number one
priority would be to stay healthy.

00:40:34.142 --> 00:40:36.190
And I think the information
can help you do that.

00:40:36.190 --> 00:40:41.417
I think for privacy, we should try and
manage it as best we can.

00:40:41.417 --> 00:40:46.500
These days, I don't think you could
ever say anything is completely secure.

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:50.240
So even if you thought you were [LAUGH]
storing it in a very, very safe place,

00:40:50.240 --> 00:40:55.790
there's a distinct chance that it will get
hacked and somebody may have access to it.

00:40:55.790 --> 00:40:59.280
And that's why we'd like to avoid
any discrimination based on

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:00.450
this sort of information.

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:02.848
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks.

00:41:02.848 --> 00:41:05.001
This is very informative and

00:41:05.001 --> 00:41:09.590
I think everyone can really take
from this talk so thank you.

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:13.660
So again, if you like this topic and
you're interested in more information,

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:18.910
this webinar is presented by the Stanford
Genetics and Genomics Certificate Program.

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:22.680
Visit geneticscertificate.stanford.edu for
more information.

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:24.914
Thank you everyone for listening.

00:41:24.914 --> 00:41:25.939
&gt;&gt; Bye all.

